//! What a generated grammar is compiled against: the runtime phase.
//!
//! A file the `codegen` module writes is a set of types that read text, and
//! reading text needs one thing of the crate that ends up holding them: a stream
//! the types may be written over. That is all this module is, and all it knows —
//! these items are what the *described* language leans on, while a spec is read,
//! analyzed and generated by the build phase, which is a different set of
//! dependencies altogether.
//!
//! Two other things a generated file leans on are deliberately absent:
//!
//! - what a `<NAME>` in a pattern calls. Such a name is an external link, known
//!   only to whoever ran the generator, who says name by name what each one
//!   calls. Unsyn ships no implementation of any name and reaches no path of its
//!   own for one, so the call lands wherever the caller's table keeps it.
//! - where a word of the described language ends. Whether `'let'` is refused
//!   inside `letter` is a fact about the language the spec is written for, so it
//!   belongs in the spec, and a generated file has no keyword boundary that this
//!   crate gave it.
//!
//! The phases are the two features that say so, `serde` being a third and a
//! build-phase one:
//!
//! - `runtime`, this module: what generated code links against, needing only a
//!   character stream of [`parserc`].
//! - `codegen`: the reader, the analyzer and the generator, needing `quote` and
//!   `proc-macro2` and nothing of this module, since what it takes from here is
//!   only the names this module's items go by once they are written down.
//!
//! A crate that keeps a generated file and never produces one therefore takes
//! this and leaves the defaults off, which is the case the split is there to
//! serve:
//!
//! ```toml
//! [dependencies]
//! unsyn = { version = "0.13", default-features = false, features = ["runtime"] }
//! ```
//!
//! The generator promises to write nothing of this crate into a file beyond the
//! bound of [`GeneratedInput`], which is what makes that promise checkable: the
//! same split holds in the other direction, since [`GeneratedInput`] is
//! error-agnostic and so a generated grammar reports failures in its own error
//! type rather than in the DSL's.

mod input;

pub use input::GeneratedInput;
